# Form Requests and policies

Controllers stay thin: validation in Form Requests, authorization in policies, business logic in services or actions, data access through Eloquent relationships rather than manual joins.

## Form Requests

- New `store`, `update`, and `upload` actions use a class in `app/Http/Requests/`. Small index filters and bulk-id lists may keep an inline `validate()`.
- Normalize input (camelCase aliases, slug uniquifying) in `prepareForValidation()`.
- `authorize()` uses the same policy ability as the controller (`create`, `update`, `upload`).
- Sanitize HTML and persist in the controller or a service, after `$request->validated()`.

## Policies

- `view` / `create` / `update` may share one manage permission.
- `delete`, `bulkDelete`, `restore`, and system tools need their own permission or a narrower role — never `return $this->viewAny($user)`.
- Register via auto-discovery (`app/Policies/{Model}Policy.php`) or `Gate::policy()` in `AppServiceProvider::boot()`; Laravel 11+ has no `$policies` property there.
- Tightening existing delete rights is a product decision; don't change old policies unless asked.
